import json, glob, os

BASE = "/home/hatch/workspace/apex-audit/docs/university/data/courses/stories"

CODAS = {
"il-16-machine-learning-ai-engineering-story-1": '''The 1962 match deserves its coda: Samuel's program beat Connecticut champion Robert Nealey in a public game — and Samuel was honest about the limits, noting the program still couldn't touch masters. The honesty is the lesson: he measured, reported, and named the gap. The field he named has been closing such gaps ever since, one benchmark at a time. Your workflow in this module is that same discipline — frame, train, evaluate honestly, deploy — scaled to modern data.''',
"il-16-machine-learning-ai-engineering-story-2": '''Lee Sedol's response became part of the legend: after the match he said AlphaGo had shown him his own understanding of Go was flawed, and he retired from professional play in 2019, calling AI "an entity that cannot be defeated." DeepMind's AlphaGo Zero (2017) then learned purely from self-play, no human games at all. The template stuck: intuition from networks, planning from search, improvement from self-play — the same three ingredients behind today's agents.''',
"il-16-machine-learning-ai-engineering-story-3": '''The geometry kept generalizing: researchers soon embedded sentences, paragraphs, images, and code — and the Transformer, four years later, made embeddings its first layer. Every token your models process begins life as a vector in Mikolov's tradition. When you tune embedding models in this module, remember 2013: the breakthrough wasn't bigger math, it was the decision to keep the hidden layer and throw away the predictions.''',
"il-17-agentic-ai-engineering-story-1": '''The test's most durable legacy may be unintended: it framed AI evaluation as deception detection, and the field has spent decades building better frames — from Winograd schemas to HELM to agentic benchmarks that measure what systems do, not how they seem. Turing's paper invites the revision; he argued we should evaluate machines as we evaluate minds, by behavior over time. Your evaluation harnesses in this module are the modern answer to his 1950 challenge.''',
"il-17-agentic-ai-engineering-story-2": '''Minsky's agencies have concrete modern forms: a planner that decomposes the goal, executors with tool access, critics that verify — the patterns this module teaches under names like orchestration and debate. The failure modes are social too: agents that agree too readily, planners that never verify, societies that converge confidently on the wrong answer. Minsky's frame predicts these pathologies, because he was describing organizations, not just minds. Build your multi-agent systems like institutions: with checks, balances, and dissent designed in.''',
"il-17-agentic-ai-engineering-story-3": '''The industry's response was swift and permanent: input filtering, output classifiers, constitutional training, red-teaming as a discipline — the entire trust stack you'll learn in this module was built in Tay's shadow. The deeper lesson is about learning loops: any agent that learns from its environment inherits the environment's adversaries. Design the loop assuming attack, bound what the agent can learn and from whom, and never let user input become training signal without a filter in between.''',
"il-17-agentic-ai-engineering-story-4": '''Newell and Simon's loop survived every paradigm shift: the General Problem Solver generalized it, expert systems ritualized it, and today's agents run it with language models as the heuristic function. The 1956 insight that endures is architectural, not algorithmic — intelligence as search guided by judgment, with the world as the feedback channel. When your agent plans, acts, observes, and replans in this module, you're running the Logic Theorist's loop at internet scale. The theorems changed; the loop didn't.''',
"il-18-technical-leadership-interview-prep-story-1": '''The Garmisch report's recommendations — better specification, measurement, and management — became the next two decades' research agenda, from structured programming to formal methods. The irony the industry still lives with: software did become an engineering discipline, but the craft never fully surrendered to the process. The TPM's job, then and now, is the balance Garmisch named — enough process to ship reliably, enough craft to solve the unknown. Your interview answers should show you can hold both.''',
"il-18-technical-leadership-interview-prep-story-2": '''The scoring discipline is what most companies get wrong: Grove insisted that a 0.7 on a stretch goal beats a 1.0 on a safe one, because the point is ambition, not compliance. Teams that tie OKRs to compensation get sandbagging; teams that tie them to learning get honesty. In your leadership interviews, the OKR story signals that you understand metrics as instruments of alignment, not surveillance — the distinction this module's judgment training is built on.''',
"il-18-technical-leadership-interview-prep-story-3": '''The rituals ossified into certification industries, and Sutherland himself has warned against the cargo cult — stand-ups without inspection, sprints without adaptation. The 1995 paper's actual claim was modest: empirical process control beats defined process for complex work. Keep that sentence in your pocket for interviews: when they ask how you run teams, the answer is the principle, not the rituals.''',
"il-19-multicloud-devops-ai-bootcamp-story-1": '''Git's data model is the reason it won: everything is a content-addressed object, history is a directed acyclic graph, and branches are just movable pointers — simple enough to reason about, powerful enough to scale to millions of commits. GitHub's 2008 insight was social, not technical: make the graph visible, add pull requests, and development becomes collaboration. In this module you'll learn the model first and the commands second, because the model is what makes the commands make sense.''',
"il-19-multicloud-devops-ai-bootcamp-story-2": '''The Dockerfile's genius was declarative simplicity: a readable recipe anyone could audit, version, and share — infrastructure as text, years before the phrase was standard. The registry completed the loop, making images distributable like packages. For the DevOps engineer, the lesson generalizes: adoption follows the developer experience, not the technology. When you containerize in this module, you're practicing the packaging discipline that turned a kernel feature into an industry.''',
"il-19-multicloud-devops-ai-bootcamp-story-3": '''The state file became the profession's shared obsession — and its sharpest lessons: state is the source of truth the plan diffs against, so backends, locking, and drift detection aren't trivia, they're the job. Terraform's provider ecosystem turned it into a universal adapter. In this module you'll learn the full discipline: modules for reuse, workspaces for environments, and the humility to run plan before apply, every single time.''',
"il-19-multicloud-devops-ai-bootcamp-story-4": '''The Phoenix Project (2013) novelized the movement for managers, and the "three ways" — flow, feedback, continual learning — gave it doctrine. But Debois's original insight remains the sharpest: the wall between dev and ops was organizational, and tools alone don't demolish it. Your bootcamp starts with culture because 2009's lesson endures: automate the pipeline, but integrate the people first.''',
"il-20-mathematics-story-1": '''Shannon's 1948 paper also gave us the noisy-channel coding theorem — the proof that reliable communication over unreliable channels is possible at any rate below capacity — which is why your phone works in a tunnel. Two papers, twelve years apart, one twenty-year-old's intuition compounded: first make logic physical, then make information measurable. Count carefully in this module; the bit is watching.''',
"il-20-mathematics-story-2": '''Boole's work fed directly into the logicism of Frege and Russell — the attempt to ground all mathematics in logic — and his daughter Mary Everest Boole became a noted mathematics educator. The algebra waited seventy years for its application, then became the most-applied mathematics in history. In this module you'll meet Boolean algebra as pure structure; remember that the purest structures find the widest uses, usually decades later.''',
"il-20-mathematics-story-3": '''The priority war's collateral damage was real: British mathematics, loyal to Newton's fluxions, fell behind the continent for a century — a cautionary tale about letting nationalism steer notation. Rigor arrived in the 1800s when Cauchy and Weierstrass rebuilt calculus on limits, answering Berkeley's ghosts at last. What you learn in this module is their cleaned-up version: the farm boy's insight, the diplomat's notation, the analysts' rigor, compounded.''',
"il-20-mathematics-story-4": '''Quetelet's "average man" had a dark twin: the same statistics were soon used to rank human worth, a history the ethical analyst must know. The curve itself is innocent — the central limit theorem guarantees it wherever many small effects add up — but its applications never are. In this module you'll wield the normal distribution constantly; wield it knowing both its power and its past.''',
"il-20-mathematics-story-5": '''Euler's "trifle" now underpins the module's hardest problems: the traveling salesman on graphs, network flows, the PageRank you'll meet elsewhere in this program. The Königsberg walk is still the right first proof to learn — it teaches the move that defines the field: strip away the geography, keep the connections. Dots and lines, 1736 to today.''',
"il-21-humanities-story-1": '''Popper's criterion took hits — Kuhn, Feyerabend, and the historians showed science is messier than the logic — but as a working discipline it survives everywhere: the severe test is still the gold standard, from particle physics to A/B testing. For the technologist, the takeaway is practical, not philosophical: design the test that could prove you wrong, and run it first. The beliefs that survive are the only ones you've earned.''',
"il-21-humanities-story-2": '''The book's most controversial claim — incommensurability, the idea that rival paradigms can't fully communicate — drew fire from Popper and others, but the core pattern stuck: normal science, crisis, revolution. Watch for it in your own field: the anomalies accumulating at the edges, the young researchers ignoring the old rules, the sudden flip. Kuhn gave you the diagnostic; the history of science in this module gives you the case studies.''',
"il-21-humanities-story-3": '''Smith's pin factory had a shadow he acknowledged: the worker confined to one operation becomes, in his words, "as stupid and ignorant as it is possible for a human creature to become" — which is why he argued for public education. The Wealth of Nations is subtler than its slogans. In this module you'll learn the machinery — markets, costs, incentives — but read Smith whole: the founder of economics worried about what the division of labor does to the laborer.''',
"il-21-humanities-story-4": '''The lectures' famous difficulty is the point: Feynman refused to simplify, only to clarify — and generations of physicists learned that clarity is the harder achievement. His technique for learning — explain it simply, find the gaps, repeat — is this module's method for writing. If your explanation needs jargon to stand up, it doesn't stand up. Rebuild it until a freshman could follow.''',
"il-22-esoteric-secret-knowledge-story-1": '''The Lurianic system built on the Zohar — tzimtzum (divine withdrawal), shevirat ha-kelim (the shattering of the vessels), tikkun (repair) — gave Kabbalah its most influential cosmology, flowing into Hasidism and modern spiritual literature. Approach the text as its tradition does: not as a book to finish, but as a field to enter. The symbols repay the patience this module is designed to build.''',
"il-22-esoteric-secret-knowledge-story-2": '''The experimental habit alchemy drilled — heat it, distill it, write down what happened — survived the theory's death and became chemistry's method. Brand's phosphorus, the mineral acids, the distillation apparatus: the wrong quest built the right instruments. In this module, study alchemy as the history of a productive error — and notice how often the symbols say something true about transformation, even when the lead stayed lead.''',
"il-22-esoteric-secret-knowledge-story-3": '''Smith's illustrations did what no occult text had: they made every card readable at a glance — the Three of Swords' heart, the Tower's lightning — turning an elite system into a vernacular one. That's why her deck conquered the world while fancier ones faded. In this module, learn the Waite-Smith images as a visual language with a real history: a game, a forgery of antiquity, a Golden Dawn filing system, and an artist's genius, layered.''',
"il-22-esoteric-secret-knowledge-story-4": '''Andreae, the likely author, later distanced himself — calling the whole thing a "ludibrium," a joke — but the joke had already organized a movement. The pattern is the lesson: the manifestos functioned as hyperstition, a fiction that summoned its own believers. In this module, treat Rosicrucianism as the case study in how esoteric ideas propagate — not by proof, but by promising that the real teaching is always one initiation further in.''',
"il-22-esoteric-secret-knowledge-story-5": '''The order's papers — the rituals, the knowledge lectures, the Enochian workings — leaked so thoroughly that modern ceremonial magic is essentially Golden Dawn material with better production values. Yeats stayed loyal for decades; Crowley turned the schism into Thelema. Learn the system as the great synthesis it was, and the politics as the warning it remains: the most successful magical order in history lasted barely a dozen years before human nature dissolved it.''',
"il-23-frontier-knowledge-story-1": '''The patent war — Berkeley versus the Broad Institute — ran for years and reshaped how universities value biotech IP, while the science marched on regardless. The first approved therapies (for sickle cell, 2023) proved the medicine real. In this module, CRISPR is the template for reading any frontier: the mechanism, the timeline from paper to product, and the governance question that arrives with the capability. The scissors don't decide; we do.''',
"il-23-frontier-knowledge-story-2": '''The engineering war since has been against decoherence — superconducting loops, trapped ions, topological bets — with error correction as the long game: thousands of physical qubits per logical one. Feynman's 1981 point stands untouched: the universe computes quantumly, and matching its mathematics is the only way to simulate it. In this module you'll learn the qubit as physics first, hype last — the frontier method applied to the hardest hardware problem alive.''',
"il-23-frontier-knowledge-story-3": '''The private race is the new chapter: Commonwealth Fusion, Helion, and dozens of startups have raised billions on approaches NIF never tried — compact tokamaks, field-reversed configurations — betting that the physics proof unlocks engineering sprints. Whether any of them reaches the grid is the open question this module tracks. Ignition proved the mountain climbable; the startups are arguing about the route.''',
"il-23-frontier-knowledge-story-4": '''The law's real content was economic as much as technical: each generation halved the cost per transistor, funding the next generation's fab. That flywheel — now straining at $20 billion per fab — is why the frontier bets matter: chiplets, 3D stacking, photonics, new architectures. In this module you'll learn to read the post-Moore landscape the way Moore read 1965: as data, not destiny.''',
"il-24-threatlocker-assessment-prep-cbt-story-1": '''The Cyber Hero model is the operational expression of the founding bet: if you block by default, customers need fast, expert humans to approve the legitimate exceptions — so support becomes part of the product, not a cost center. For your assessment, internalize the triage logic the story implies: default-deny first, allowlist deliberately, ringfence what you allow. The company's history is the answer key to its interview.''',
"il-24-threatlocker-assessment-prep-cbt-story-2": '''The 1975 paper's eighth principle — psychological acceptability — is the one practitioners forget and users pay for: security that fights its users gets bypassed. ThreatLocker's Learning Mode is the modern answer, baselining the legitimate before enforcing the deny. In this CBT you'll practice the full cycle: observe, baseline, enforce, contain. Saltzer and Schroeder wrote the principles; your triage decisions are the implementation.''',
"il-24-threatlocker-assessment-prep-cbt-story-3": '''The model's military origin is worth remembering: it adapts the targeting cycle (find, fix, track, target, engage, assess) to network defense — the defender's kill chain breaks the attacker's. Its limitation taught the field the next lesson: real intrusions loop and branch, which is why your attack-graph module exists. Use the chain for the narrative, the graph for the analysis, ATT&CK for the vocabulary.''',
"il-24-threatlocker-assessment-prep-cbt-story-4": '''Price, who read the paper, was himself remarkable — a minister, a mathematician, and the intellectual godfather of American independence through his pamphlets. The theorem's modern revenge is total: spam filters, medical diagnosis, machine learning, and your detection metrics all run on 1763. In the assessment, when you weigh an alert, you're Price's reader: updating beliefs with evidence, against the base rate, correctly.''',
"il-25-cybersecurity-professional-certificate-story-1": '''The secrecy lasted thirty years — Colossus's engineers were ordered to destroy the machines and stay silent, so the computer's birth certificate was sealed until the 1970s. When it emerged, computing history had to be rewritten: the programmable electronic computer was, in part, a codebreaking machine. Your certificate's cryptography module starts here because everything since — from DES to AES — is the same story with new math.''',
"il-25-cybersecurity-professional-certificate-story-2": '''The Belgian winners were the underdogs: Rijndael beat better-funded finalists on elegance and speed, and Daemen and Rijmen gave the design away freely. The contest's openness was the point — NIST wanted a cipher the world could trust because the world had attacked it. Two decades without a practical break is the verdict. In this module you'll implement AES modes and learn why the contest model, not the cipher alone, is what you're actually trusting.''',
"il-25-cybersecurity-professional-certificate-story-3": '''The attribution — widely reported as a US-Israeli operation — was never officially confirmed, which is itself the lesson: cyberweapons live in the gray zone between espionage and war, with no declared rules. The Natanz centrifuges were the target; the precedent was the payload. In this module you'll learn OT risk assessment for the world Stuxnet revealed: air gaps as delay, PLCs as targets, physical consequences as the stakes.''',
"il-25-cybersecurity-professional-certificate-story-4": '''The crypto wars' first battle set the industry's permanent position: exceptional access breaks the mathematics for everyone, because a backdoor is just a vulnerability with a badge. The fight recurs — Apple vs. FBI in 2016, messaging encryption today — with the same 1993 arguments. In your governance module, Clipper is the precedent: policy must respect what the math allows, or the math routes around the policy.''',
"il-26-data-analyst-certification-senior-mastery-story-1": '''Playfair's charts were hand-engraved, his data gathered by correspondence — and his atlas flopped commercially, like most of his ventures. The ideas outlived the businessman: two centuries later, every BI tool ships his three charts as defaults. In this module you'll learn when to use each — bars for comparison, lines for time, pies sparingly — the visual grammar he invented, still spoken.''',
"il-26-data-analyst-certification-senior-mastery-story-2": '''The reforms her diagram drove — sanitation, ventilation, record-keeping — cut military death rates and reshaped public health; she then applied the same statistical method to Indian sanitation and hospital design for decades. The coxcomb was advocacy disguised as analysis, and it worked because the analysis was impeccable. In this module, when you build the dashboard for stakeholders, remember 1858: the chart is not the decoration of the argument, it is the argument.''',
"il-26-data-analyst-certification-senior-mastery-story-3": '''Galton's legacy is double-edged — the same man founded eugenics, and his statistics were built partly to rank human worth — a history the ethical analyst must know outright. The mathematics is sound; the uses were not always. Regression itself is innocent: the tendency of extremes to moderate. In this module you'll fit regressions constantly; fit them knowing both the power and the provenance, and never let the model do the moral reasoning.''',
"il-26-data-analyst-certification-senior-mastery-story-4": '''The pseudonym lasted: "Student's t-test" is still the name, and Gosset published under it for his entire career, rising to head brewer while his test conquered science. Fisher corresponded with him extensively — the two shaped modern statistics together, the brewer and the professor. In this module, the t-test is your daily tool for small samples; use it with Gosset's practicality — the question was always about the barley, not the mathematics.''',
"il-27-it-infrastructure-specialist-threatlocker-mastery-story-1": '''The Times buried the announcement on page 46 — the editors couldn't know the sliver of germanium would matter more than anything else in the paper that day. Kilby's integrated circuit (1958) and Noyce's planar process (1959) compounded it into the chip; the rest is the digital age. In this module you'll handle the descendants: seat the CPU, respect the silicon, and remember that every server you rack is 1947, miniaturized a billionfold.''',
"il-27-it-infrastructure-specialist-threatlocker-mastery-story-2": '''Feinler's NIC ran on human labor — emails, phone calls, a text file — and Mockapetris's genius was seeing that names needed a protocol, not a clerk. The hierarchy he designed delegated authority the way the internet delegates everything: no center, just agreements. Kaminsky's 2008 cache-poisoning revelation showed the design's age, and DNSSEC has been the slow fix. In this module you'll run DNS as infrastructure and defend it as a target — the 1983 protocol, still resolving, still under attack.''',
"il-27-it-infrastructure-specialist-threatlocker-mastery-story-3": '''Greenberg's reporting made the Ghana detail immortal: the domain controller survived because a power cut took it offline at the right hour — luck, not planning, saved the rebuild. The module's lesson is to replace luck with design: offline backups, immutable copies, rehearsed restores. NotPetya didn't care about Maersk's plans; the Ghana server didn't need them. Build so you don't need luck.''',
"il-27-it-infrastructure-specialist-threatlocker-mastery-story-4": '''Rosenblum's binary-translation trick was deemed impossible by the experts — x86 was "unvirtualizable" — until VMware shipped it and the experts updated their theories. The pattern recurs in infrastructure: the constraint is usually imagination, not physics. In this module you'll administer Hyper-V knowing the lineage — IBM's 1967 fiction, VMware's 1998 product, the cloud's foundation — and the lesson: virtualize boldly, but understand the layer you're standing on.''',
"il-28-devsecops-cloud-infrastructure-mastery-story-1": '''The IETF standardization (SSH-2) and OpenBSD's clean-room OpenSSH (1999) completed the takeover: one free, audited implementation everywhere, replacing telnet in every datacenter within years. In this module you'll manage keys, agents, and tunnels daily — and every time you refuse a password login for key-only auth, you're enforcing 1995's lesson: the wire is hostile, the endpoints must prove themselves.''',
"il-28-devsecops-cloud-infrastructure-mastery-story-2": '''The kernel primitives — namespaces for isolation, cgroups for limits — were built over a decade by engineers solving hyperscale problems before Docker packaged them for everyone. The 2013 breakthrough was distribution, not invention: the Dockerfile made the primitive legible, the registry made it shippable. In this module you'll harden what Docker popularized: scan the image, drop the privileges, sign the artifact. The container is the package; the hardening is the product.''',
"il-28-devsecops-cloud-infrastructure-mastery-story-3": '''The remediation became a case study in dependency blindness: organizations found Log4j nested five layers deep in vendor products, with no inventory to find it — the SBOM mandate that followed (EO 14028) was the policy response. In this module you'll build the shift-left pipeline that would have caught it: scan on commit, inventory always, patch fast. The next one's already in someone's tree.''',
"il-28-devsecops-cloud-infrastructure-mastery-story-4": '''The SVR's tradecraft was the story's second half: the backdoor slept for weeks, checked its surroundings, and only activated for chosen targets — supply-chain compromise with intelligence-service patience. The response reshaped the industry: EO 14028's SBOM mandates, SLSA's provenance framework, the new rule that build systems are critical infrastructure. In this module you'll implement those defenses: signed artifacts, verified provenance, minimal trust. The update channel is the perimeter now.''',
"il-29-threatlocker-ai-cybersecurity-mastery-story-1": '''The mitigations stacked up over two decades — StackGuard's canaries (1998), non-executable stacks, ASLR, and finally memory-safe languages — each answering the previous generation of exploits, each bypassed in turn. The 6.858 modules in this course teach the full arc: how the attack works, why each defense helps, where each fails. Aleph One's tutorial is still the clearest starting point ever written; the defenses are the rest of the course.''',
"il-29-threatlocker-ai-cybersecurity-mastery-story-2": '''The funding response was the structural fix: the Core Infrastructure Initiative, backed by the biggest tech companies, poured resources into OpenSSL and its peers — an admission that the internet's most critical code had been maintained by volunteers. In this module, when you configure TLS, carry 2014's two lessons: audit the implementation, not just the protocol — and fund the infrastructure you depend on.''',
"il-29-threatlocker-ai-cybersecurity-mastery-story-3": '''The computation — 110 GPU-years — was the point: collisions had moved from theory to budget line, and any well-funded attacker could replicate it. Browsers had already begun distrusting SHA-1 certificates; Git began its long migration to SHA-256. In this module you'll learn hash functions as residents with leases — and SHAttered is the eviction notice, framed.''',
"il-29-threatlocker-ai-cybersecurity-mastery-story-4": '''The framework's power is compositional: techniques chain into procedures, procedures into campaigns, and the matrix gives every step a name, a detection, and a mitigation. Vendors, intel teams, and SOCs now speak it natively. In this module you'll map intrusions to the matrix until it's reflexive: see the behavior, name the technique, deploy the detection. MITRE gave the industry the playbook; fluency is the job.''',
}

updated = 0
for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(BASE, "il-*.json"))):
    if f.endswith("_index.json"):
        continue
    d = json.load(open(f))
    changed = False
    for s in d["stories"]:
        coda = CODAS.get(s["id"])
        if coda:
            s["narrative"] = s["narrative"].rstrip() + " " + coda
            changed = True
            updated += 1
    if changed:
        json.dump(d, open(f, "w"), ensure_ascii=False, indent=1)

print("codas applied:", updated)

bad = []
for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(BASE, "il-*.json"))):
    if f.endswith("_index.json"):
        continue
    d = json.load(open(f))
    for s in d["stories"]:
        w = len(s["narrative"].split())
        if not (300 <= w <= 600):
            bad.append((s["id"], w))
print("still out of range:", bad if bad else "none")
